                <section class="container-fluid col-lg-10 py-5">
                    <div class="row">
                        <div class="col">

                            <h2 class="mb-3 text-center nwe-toke-txt-4" data-aos="fade-up">
                                How Does Clarisco's Handle <span class="curve-text">Game Porting</span> Without Fail?
                            </h2>

                            <p class="nw-w-txt-2 text-center mb-3" data-aos="fade-up" style="color: #0000008f;">
                                Clarisco has a named porting lead who owns the milestone, manages the platform holder
                                relationship, and escalates technical blockers without competing priorities diverting
                                their
                                attention. This matters most during platform certification.
                            </p>

                            <p class="nw-w-txt-2 text-center mb-3" data-aos="fade-up" style="color: #0000008f;">
                                First-party certification processes include PlayStation TRC, Xbox TCR, and Nintendo
                                Switch
                                LotCheck, which generate iterative feedback from platform holders and require faster
                                response times to avoid submission delays.
                            </p>

                            <p class="nw-w-txt-2 text-center mb-3" data-aos="fade-up" style="color: #0000008f;">
                                When a studio's internal engineers own that process alongside a live development
                                roadmap,
                                response turnaround slows and cert cycles compound. When Clarisco owns the certification
                                process, the platform holder has a dedicated point of contact who treats the cert cycle
                                as
